  "request_sha256": "2d4426b4476f40c6a9748fbf0c53a075fc9682018c65b1697a4bd3aa34d07881",
  "response": {
    "data": {
      "A": "Research the low-overhead sequential DP. Use one in-place row: initialize dp[0]=1, reset the left carry at each row, set both dp[j] and the carry to zero at obstacles, otherwise add the old dp[j] to the carry. Replace remainder by one conditional subtraction: two normalized residues sum to at most 2*MOD-2, which fits signed 32-bit arithmetic. Eliminate row copies and evaluate unsynchronized buffered input. Investigate O(N+M) obstacle storage with sorted, deduplicated columns per row; process obstacle-free spans without per-cell bitmap checks. Include parsing, sorting and allocation in measurements rather than assuming this representation wins. Preserve arbitrary input order, repeated coordinates, blocked endpoints and N=1. Deliver a simple exact fallback and independently measured improvements.",
      "B": "Research dependency-aware acceleration on ARM64, independently of the sparse algorithm. Primary direction: square tiles scheduled by anti-diagonals, with each tile consuming north and west boundary vectors and producing south and east boundaries. Use tile-local rolling rows and a rolling boundary frontier, not an N-by-N DP allocation. Evaluate persistent workers, at most four, only through facilities supported by the unchanged build. Tune granularity and retain a sequential path when synchronization exceeds useful work; never parallelize dependent rows directly. As a separate candidate, evaluate ARM64 NEON inclusive modular prefix scans within obstacle-free spans. Compute local vector prefixes before adding the incoming carry, reduce after additions, reset carry at obstacles, and handle tails exactly. Measure threading and SIMD separately before combining them; do not require SVE, GPU support or compiler changes.",
      "C": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle combinatorial solver. Sort and deduplicate blocked coordinates, append the unblocked destination, and process points in row/column order. For q=(y,x), compute w(q)=binom(y+x,y) minus the sum over earlier coordinate-wise preceding blocked points p of w(p)*binom((y-p.y)+(x-p.x),y-p.y), modulo MOD. This subtracts paths by their first blocked point. Precompute factorials and inverse factorials through 2*N-2; this bound is below MOD. Use 64-bit products and normalized modular subtraction. Handle blocked origin/destination explicitly; zero obstacles reduce exactly to binom(2*N-2,N-1). Storage should be O(N+U), not O(U squared), where U is the unique obstacle count. Because work is O(N+M log M+U squared), retain an exact grid-DP fallback. Establish the crossover from measured costs and general workload properties, not particular test values; sparse density alone does not make the quadratic obstacle algorithm cheap.",
      "G": "Integrate only validated programs produced by A/B/C. Preserve standalone winners and compare complete implementations and minimal combinations under the unchanged harness. Consider a dispatcher using mathematically justified endpoint/empty-obstacle cases, a measured cost comparison for C versus grid DP, and a measured serial/parallel choice within grid DP. Account for preprocessing and avoid paying for factorial tables, sorting or thread startup on paths that do not need them. An integrated program can be slower than either component: retain combinations only when repeated measurements support them, and keep the best standalone option otherwise. Require exact modulo results for every valid input, including N=1, arbitrary or repeated obstacle coordinates, complete barriers, blocked endpoints, vector tails and partial tiles. Preserve standard input/output, tests, oracle, compiler settings, target, permissions and budgets. Report measured whole-program performance without extrapolating additive speedups.",
      "analysis": "All roles target Problem D, identified by the supplied source; A/B/C are research-role labels, not the other contest problems. The baseline is correct on four supplied checks and averages 0.0786307 seconds across three runs. There are no optimized measurements, workload dimensions or profiles, so no particular bottleneck or obstacle distribution is established. The source performs quadratic grid traversal and blocked-cell storage, per-cell remainder operations, packed-bit accesses and row copies. Investigate three complementary directions: cheaper exact DP, dependency-aware CPU acceleration, and an asymptotically different sparse solver with fallback. The fixed target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU. At this measured runtime, preprocessing and synchronization costs must be included rather than treated as negligible."
    },
